Ported from the sam-3d-objects-ggml upstream ggml tree (third_party/ggml
unary.cuh, submodule 8599e0ea): make the CUDA SiLU activation use precise
device math instead of the fast-math approximation.

Rationale: with GGML_CUDA_FAST_MATH the generic `x / (1.0f + expf(-x))`
form lowers to __fdividef/__expf, which perturbs F16 activation outputs by
ULPs. The sam3d diffusion samplers amplify those ULP flips into a real
occupancy gap (~6% gaussian count divergence vs the upstream reference),
so the upstream project forces the numerator/denominator onto the precise
device-math path. Applied globally to match upstream exactly; non-sam3d
accuracy gates are tolerance-based and precise math only moves them closer
to the PyTorch reference.

--- a/src/ggml-cuda/unary.cuh
+++ b/src/ggml-cuda/unary.cuh
@@ -94,7 +94,11 @@
 void ggml_cuda_op_relu_sqr(ggml_backend_cuda_context & ctx, ggml_tensor * relu_node, ggml_tensor * sqr_node);
 
 __device__ __forceinline__ float ggml_cuda_op_silu_single(float x) {
-    return x / (1.0f + expf(-x));
+    // CUDA's -use_fast_math lowers both the division and expf. PyTorch's F16
+    // SiLU rounds the F32 expression before the F16 store, so keep its
+    // numerator and denominator on the precise device-math path.
+    const float denominator = 1.0f + static_cast<float>(exp(static_cast<double>(-x)));
+    return __fdiv_rn(x, denominator);
 }
 
 __device__ __forceinline__ float ggml_cuda_op_gelu_single(float x) {
